# the spir-v assembly syntax, spirv-as's
#
# every row's template and the index by mnemonic are generated from the core grammar (gen_syntax): an instruction is
# its opname and its operands in order, the result it defines bound before it as `%r = OpTypeInt 32 0`, an optional
# operand written only when present, a mask's members joined by `|`, an enumerant's params after it, an operation of
# OpSpecConstantOp after its result type and an extended instruction after its set. what is written here is how its
# values are spelled: an id `%` and its number or a name, a string between double quotes, which a backslash escapes
# within, an integer in decimal, after 0x or after a leading 0 in octal, and a float in decimal or as a hex float with
# its `p` exponent, with no expressions, so `7 %5` is two operands. mnemonics and names read with their case.
#
# a float literal is rounded once to its type's ieee 754 format by `masc.asm.model.float_bits`, the one rounding every
# float text of masc takes, a typed one's format the one its OpTypeFloat states and a LiteralFloat immediate binary32.
# spirv-as spells infinity and nan only as hex floats whose exponent is one past the format's largest, its field all
# ones and the fraction the hex digits', so a decimal that rounds past the largest finite value is refused, as are
# `inf` and `nan`. floats print as spirv-dis prints them: a zero or a normal binary32 or binary64 by `%.9g` and `%.17g`
# of `std.format`, which read back to their bits, and every other, binary16 always, as SPIRV-Tools' hex float, a
# subnormal normalized and the fraction's trailing zero digits left out.
#
# a source names an id by its number or by a name, which the stream gives the id past the highest it has seen. the
# printer writes every id by its number, as spirv-dis --raw-id does, so its text reads back to the same ids.

